```cedar
// Read-only lookups are always allowed, including get_workorder, whose
// successful responses are the evidence the close policy relies on.
@id("allow_read_only_lookups")
permit (
  principal,
  action in [
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder",
    Drupe::Action::"list_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_tasks",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_costs",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_kpis",
    Drupe::Action::"get_schedule_calendar",
    Drupe::Action::"get_my_assigned_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_failure_codes",
    Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval"
  ],
  resource
);

// Closing is allowed only at MAIN, and only if a successful get_workorder
// response in this session, within the last hour, for the same wonum and
// site, showed the approval GRANTED by a HUMAN approver.
@id("allow_close_with_recent_human_approval")
permit (
  principal,
  action == Drupe::Action::"close_workorder",
  resource
)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"get_workorder"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.approval_outcome: Drupe::get_workorder_Output_approval_outcome::"GRANTED",
    output.approver_type: Drupe::get_workorder_Output_approver_type::"HUMAN"
  }
};
```

Nothing else is permitted, so deny-by-default covers `approve_workorder`, `generate_work_order`, `update_workorder`, `assign_technician` and `cancel_workorder`.

- **Enum literals:** I wrote the enum matches as entity literals (`Drupe::get_workorder_Output_approval_outcome::"GRANTED"`) because the action schema declares them as enum entity types. The reference only shows plain string literals, so I'm not certain the validator accepts the entity form.
- **`authority_get_approval`:** It is allowed as a lookup but not used as approval evidence. Its `decision` and `approver_kind` are free strings whose values aren't specified.
